              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Keep a note of the product and color you used, and store a sealed quart for touch-ups. Matching an old stain by eye years later is much harder than it sounds.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. When a Sealer or Paint Makes More Sense
          </h2>

          <p>
            Stain is not the only option. A clear water repellent, sometimes sold as a deck sealant, keeps moisture out of new wood and costs less per year if you are happy to reapply it annually. It is a reasonable choice for a covered porch or a new cedar deck you want to look as natural as possible for a season or two. Our <a href="/best-deck-sealant/">best deck sealant</a> guide compares the clear options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Deck paints and resurfacing coatings sit at the other end. They are thick, fill small cracks and hide a lot of damage, which makes them tempting for an old deck. But they are film-formers, and on horizontal boards they can trap moisture and peel. They work best on structurally sound boards that are completely dry and on decks with good airflow beneath.
          </p>

          <p>
            For fences, pergolas and siding, which take far less abuse than a deck floor, a stain that would last two years on the deck may last five or more. If you are finishing those too, look at our guide to <a href="/best-fence-stain/">fence stains</a> for products designed for vertical surfaces and bigger areas.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are unsure whether your boards can still hold a finish, push a screwdriver into a few suspect spots. If it sinks in easily, the wood is soft and needs replacing before you spend money on any coating.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Staining Over a Peeling Finish</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                New stain cannot bond to a coating that is already lifting. It adds weight to the failing film and peels away with it, usually within one season. Scrape or strip every loose area back to sound wood, brighten the surface and let it dry before you open a can of anything new.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Working in Full Midday Sun</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hot boards flash-dry the stain before it can soak in. The result is lap marks, uneven color and a thin surface layer that wears quickly. Start early on the shaded side, follow the shade around the house, and stop when the boards are too hot to keep your palm on.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Brightener</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cleaners and strippers leave the wood alkaline and darkened, and stain absorbs unevenly on that surface. A brightener resets the pH, removes tannin and rust marks and opens the grain. It costs little, takes minutes to apply and often makes the biggest visible difference in the finished deck.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Piling On a Heavy Second Coat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Penetrating stain only goes where the wood can absorb it. A thick second coat on saturated boards sits on top, stays tacky for days and then wears or peels unevenly. Apply thin coats, back-brush, and wipe up anything still glossy on the surface after the time listed on the label.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing Color From a Paper Chip</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The same stain looks completely different on new pine, weathered cedar and redwood. Store chips and online swatches only show a rough idea. Buy a sample, test it on a prepared board in a hidden corner, and let it dry fully before you decide on gallons for the whole deck.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When does a deck need restaining?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most decks need a maintenance coat on the floor every two to three years with a semi-transparent stain, every year with a clear or toner finish, and every three to five years with a solid stain. Sunny, south-facing decks and heavy foot traffic shorten that. When water stops beading and the high-traffic paths look faded, it is time to clean and recoat.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I choose an oil or a water-based deck stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Oil-based penetrating stains are more forgiving for most homeowners because they soak in deeply, rarely leave lap marks and fade rather than peel. Water-based stains dry faster, smell less, clean up with water and are legal in every state. A well-prepped deck can do well with either; the key is not mixing chemistries without stripping first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can fresh stain go over an older coat?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if the old stain is a penetrating type that has worn evenly and is not peeling. Clean and brighten the deck, let it dry, and apply the same or a compatible product. If the old finish is a solid stain or film that is peeling, strip or sand the loose areas first. Switching from oil to water-based usually requires stripping.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best application method for deck stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Spraying or rolling is fastest for the floor, but always back-brush right afterward to work the stain into the grain and even out puddles. A brush alone gives the best penetration on small decks. Pads work well on smooth boards. Whatever you use, work two or three boards at a time and finish each pass at a board end.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should I wait to stain a new deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the wood. Kiln-dried lumber can often be stained within weeks. Wet pressure-treated pine usually needs several weeks to a few months to dry, sometimes longer in humid climates. Use the water-drop test: if drops soak in within a few minutes, the wood is ready. If they bead, wait and test again.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does deck stain protect against water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Most deck stains contain water repellents in addition to pigment, so they reduce how much moisture the boards absorb. That limits swelling, cracking and checking. Pigment adds UV protection on top. Clear sealers repel water too but offer little sun protection, which is why decks finished with them still gray.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What deck stain color lasts the longest?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mid-tone semi-transparent and semi-solid colors such as cedar, redwood and light brown tend to hold up best. They carry enough pigment to block UV while hiding the gradual wear and dirt of foot traffic. Very light or clear finishes fade fastest, and very dark solid colors can show scuffs and heat up in direct sun.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

